  <Vertical id="uk_10_s5" title="A table with no cushion">
    <Markdown id="uk_10_s5_text">
The meeting room smelled of wet coats and somebody's hot lunch. A radiator clicked below a window that would not quite shut. Grace folded a spare agenda and wedged it into the gap. The paper rattled once, then held.

Mara had booked this campaign room; this was no parliamentary committee. The speakerphone swallowed half of every sentence until Kit found its volume button.

Zofia chose the chair nearest the door. The dog-pull had strengthened when their escort left. She kept her feet still and watched Grace sit down rather than follow. The pull eased a little. She could not tell anybody what that proved.

Darwin’s voice, carried from his grave, came from beside the radiator. She turned. Only a loose thread hung from the chair back.

The Workshop's animal-service page lay between two mugs. Under its claim that all service had been voluntary, someone had drawn a neat line in pencil. The paper beside it offered spaces for a reviewer, a representative and a conclusion.

Zofia found a pen that worked. She tested it in the margin until Grace put her hand over the nib.

“Leave room,” Grace said. “We might need to write something.”
    </Markdown>
  </Vertical>

